  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Sparql Delete requests which do not specify an exact object/literal to delete are having inconsistent results depending on if the subject having its properties deleted was created inside of the current transaction or not.  ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The query in question is:",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "DELETE { <> <",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/relation"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/relation",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": "> ?obj . }",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "WHERE { <> <",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/relation"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/relation",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": "> ?obj }",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "This query should delete every property dc:relation from the object the request is made against.  However, I've run into it working differently in the following cases:",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "1) No TX involved -> dc:relations deleted",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "2) Subject and Object created out of TX, delete query in TX -> dc:relations deleted",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "3) Subject created in TX, object out of TX -> nothing deleted, no error",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "4) Subject created out of TX, object created in TX -> dc:relations deleted",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "5) Subject and object created in TX -> nothing deleted, no error",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Attached is a script (run against the one click installer) which illustrates the bug.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "I know this seems very edge case-y, but we really are running into it.  The approach that seems to work consistently in transactions is to retrieve all the existing values and then generate a delete query from that, but it can produce some rather large queries and takes 2 requests.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
