  "description": "<p>Sparql Delete requests which do not specify an exact object/literal to delete are having inconsistent results depending on if the subject having its properties deleted was created inside of the current transaction or not.  </p>\n\n<p>The query in question is:</p>\n\n<p>DELETE </p>\n{ &lt;&gt; &lt;http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/relation&gt; ?obj . }\n<p>WHERE </p>\n{ &lt;&gt; &lt;http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/relation&gt; ?obj }\n\n<p>This query should delete every property dc:relation from the object the request is made against.  However, I've run into it working differently in the following cases:<br/>\n1) No TX involved -&gt; dc:relations deleted<br/>\n2) Subject and Object created out of TX, delete query in TX -&gt; dc:relations deleted<br/>\n3) Subject created in TX, object out of TX -&gt; nothing deleted, no error<br/>\n4) Subject created out of TX, object created in TX -&gt; dc:relations deleted<br/>\n5) Subject and object created in TX -&gt; nothing deleted, no error</p>\n\n<p>Attached is a script (run against the one click installer) which illustrates the bug.</p>\n\n<p>I know this seems very edge case-y, but we really are running into it.  The approach that seems to work consistently in transactions is to retrieve all the existing values and then generate a delete query from that, but it can produce some rather large queries and takes 2 requests.</p>",
